WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.270
The Force giveth and taketh away that's the
lesson poor Mando learns in the newest chapter

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:06.240
of The Mandalorian.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.260
The episode's title "Sanctuary" sets up that
classic, bittersweet trope of all great Western

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.980
films: the cowboy rolls into a one-horse town,
fixes problems only people on the frontier

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.520
could possibly experience, and becomes beloved.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.780
We, of course, know exactly how this is going
to end, because the Mandalorian is literally

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:24.780
a man with no name.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.320
At the episode's conclusion, he's ready to
ascend once more to the stars and continue

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.490
following his compulsion to roam but it may
not be all sunsets and solitary endings after

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.830
all, because the Child has been found by one
more of the endless horde tracking him, and

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.200
sanctuary has been spoiled.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.500
We're left again with the Mandalorian and
the Child in the middle of a wistful departure

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.720
and no idea where the next chapter may lead.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.440
We can gather now that this is part of the
point of the series' first season, to keep

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:55.719
us feeling as lost and confused as our hero
must be, searching his star charts for the

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.620
next planet to stave off the inevitable.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.359
But as Mando grows more obviously attached
to his onetime bounty, and the consequences

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.880
of his betrayal of his guild begin to stack
up, how does this end in anything other than

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.380
tragedy?

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.020
The Mandalorian's fourth episode introduced
us to Cara Dune, former Rebellion soldier-turned

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.540
outlaw mercenary who is a little rougher around
the edges than Mando.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:20.740
It was a bit of a surprise to have her drop
into Mando's life, then to see him say goodbye

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.960
to her by the episode's end, when we know
we're going to see her again but honestly,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.420
that only builds all the more intrigue around
the character.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.680
"Well then… until our paths cross."

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:34.580
"Until our paths cross."

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.399
We're pleased to see she hasn't been immediately
relegated to Potential Love Interest, and

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:43.840
that she just might grow into the role of
Mando's actual butt-kicking BFF, provided

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.280
there are no last-minute betrayals on her
part.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.320
Mando may not be allowed a permanent home
with a white picket fence, but for crying

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.180
out loud, let him have friends.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.520
Cara Dune also provides a really great narrative
opportunity: an external foil for Mando's

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:58.020
feelings.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.520
Up until now, we've had to process most of
his emotions purely through his body language,

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.340
and while actor Pedro Pascal is doing a magnificent
job communicating what's going on underneath

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:12.800
his helmet with almost no dialogue, the conceit
does leave interpretive gaps by its very nature.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.480
Mando and Cara are immediately presented as
simpatico through the time-honored trope of

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.180
morally grey characters fostering friendship
through fistfights.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.120
She lives the rough-and-tumble life, too,
and understands that Mando does want a simpler

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.780
existence, though she does not understand
why he refuses it when it's offered.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.920
We're looking forward to seeing the two meet
again, and hopefully not on opposite sides

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:43.920
of some conflict.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.980
"Sanctuary" taught us quite a bit about Mando's
motivations.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.260
Earlier, we discussed the trope of cowboys
and the dusty towns they're tempted to remain

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.080
in, but jokes aside, the idea of a permanent
home is something Mando pretty clearly wants,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.460
and actively denies himself.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.480
This applies twofold in this episode: he denies
even the possibility of connecting with Omera

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.879
on a more personal level, and initially decides
to leave Baby Yoda in the care of the village

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.380
before everything goes wrong.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.340
Why?

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.080
Because of his debt to the Mandalorian people
and we now can say with certainty that the

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.380
Mandalorian is not, well, a Mandalorian.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.940
As in, not from Mandalore and the surrounding
colony planets.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:25.200
He is taught in their warrior traditions and
observes their faith, and that is clearly

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:29.599
all that matters to what we presume are the
actual Mandalorians he has previously interacted

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:34.030
with, but he was originally of another race
or heritage as a Foundling before being rescued

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.120
by the Mandalorians.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.790
No doubt he feels a huge debt to those people
for giving him his life and the best possibility

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:44.400
of surviving on the edge of the galaxy, but
there also was a rather pregnant pause before

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.840
Mando said he was happy the Mandalorians gave
him the life they did.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.500
He also initially rebuffs Cara's assertion
that leaving Baby Yoda behind in the village,

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.090
even for his own benefit, will break his tiny
green heart with the stoic response: "He'll

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.090
get over it.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:58.580
We all do."

00:03:59.080 --> 00:03:59.640
Mando.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.140
Buddy.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.260
You loaded up that big old transport with
half the guns in the parsec and that's still

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.400
less baggage than whatever's going on in that
chrome dome of yours.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.000
But that inner conflict makes sense, doesn't
it?

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:15.040
Mando wants to honor the gifts he's been given,
but if he's not of the Mandalorians as a people

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.580
and is suddenly feeling torn from the responsibility
he has acted under for many years, that could

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:22.080
create a pretty significant crisis of identity.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.840
He works incredibly hard to be what he is,
and to live up to the Mandalorian standard.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.020
What happens when life tempts him from it?

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.920
That inner conflict isn't going away, because
the Child is coming with him, and that connection

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.480
between them is only going to grow for better
or for worse.

